        "authority_source_node_id": "7ff95a69-4fbe-41a3-aecd-40a9e059b84c",
        "authority_source_quote": "No. **C and C++ should be one Lexicon adapter**, with shared ownership and output schema.\n\nInternally, it will still need separate language handling because C++ is not merely a superset that can always be parsed as C:\n\n- Separate parser/grammar selection for `.c` versus `.cc`, `.cpp`, `.cxx`, etc.\n- Shared handling for headers, with language inferred from includers, project configuration, or heuristics\n- C++-specific symbols: namespaces, classes, templates, overloads, methods, inheritance\n- C-specific constructs: tagged structs/unions, function prototypes, translation-unit linkage\n- Shared preprocessor, include graph, macros, declarations, definitions, calls, and type relationships\n- Optional `compile_commands.json` support for include paths, defines, language standard, and per-file flags\n\nSo structurally:\n\n```text\nLexicon C-family adapter\n├── common preprocessing/include handling\n├── C parser and normalization\n├── C++ parser and normalization\n└── shared C-family relationship extraction\n```\n\nThat avoids duplicated include/preprocessor logic while still acknowledging that the two grammars and semantic models differ. I would expose it as something like `c-family`, but report each file’s actual language as `c` or `cpp` in the normalized output.",
        "category": "decision",
        "content": "Lexicon uses a single shared C-family adapter for C and C++ rather than separate C and C++ adapters, with each file's language reported as c or cpp.",
        "grounding_source_conversation_id": "",
        "grounding_source_node_id": "",
        "grounding_source_quote": "",
        "lifecycle": "current",
        "source_node_id": "8bb025fb-ed6d-49c2-9449-e6c1317782d2",
        "source_quote": "Fuckit, add that",
        "title": "Lexicon uses one shared C-family adapter for C and C++",
        "type": "project"
      },
      {
        "authority_kind": "direct",
